"Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some glue sticks to spare?","Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?",7,7,7,7,"- Total glue sticks needed: 27 students × 2 each = 54
- Each pack has 8 glue sticks
- Packs needed = smallest whole number so that packs × 8 ≥ 54
- 6 packs = 48 (not enough), 7 packs = 56 (enough, with 2 spare)

#### 7","To solve this, I'll break down the problem step-by-step for clarity:

- First, calculate the total number of glue sticks needed: 27 students times 2 glue sticks each equals 54 glue sticks.
- Next, since each pack contains 8 glue sticks, I will find out how many packs are required by calculating the ceiling of 54 divided by 8.
- This gives approximately 6.75 packs, so we round up to 7 packs to ensure we have enough.
- Multiplying 8 by 7 results in 56 glue sticks, providing 2 extras.

#### 7","- Total glue sticks needed: 27 students × 2 each = 54
- Each pack has 8 glue sticks
- Packs needed = smallest whole number so that packs × 8 ≥ 54
- 6 packs = 48 (not enough), 7 packs = 56 (enough, with 2 spare)
